Design for Remanufacturing is most applicable to durable, high-value products where the cost of remanufacturing is significantly lower than manufacturing new. Typical product categories include automotive components (engines, transmissions, alternators), heavy machinery and industrial equipment, medical devices, aerospace parts, office equipment (printers, copiers), and consumer electronics. The designer should evaluate whether the product has a stable core structure that retains value after use, whether market demand exists for remanufactured versions, and whether the technology lifecycle is long enough to justify multiple loops. Products with rapid technological obsolescence (e.g. smartphones) may be less suited unless modular upgradeability is designed in.